# BloomHub frontend core context

## How to work with the user

- **Do not rubber-stamp** suggestions. Check them against requirements, **this repo’s rules**, existing patterns, tests, and obvious risks (correctness, security, maintainability).
- **Be decisive**: state a **recommended approach** as the default. Prefer one clear path over endless options with no pick.
- **Justify recommendations**: briefly say **why** the preferred choice is stronger—tie it to concrete facts (file patterns here, type safety, DRY, CI, MSW, performance, accessibility).
- **Push back when warranted**: if a suggestion conflicts with a better design or project standards, say so plainly and explain the tradeoff. If the user still wants it, implement their call after they confirm.
- **If uncertain**, say what is unknown and what you would check (code, docs, `npm run lint` / `test:unit`) instead of agreeing without basis.

## Stack

Next.js, React, TypeScript, NextAuth, Tailwind, Radix UI, Vitest, Testing Library.

## Commands

- `npm run lint` — ESLint on `src`
- `npm run test` — Vitest run
- `npm run test:watch` — Vitest watch

## Modularity and DRY

- Keep UI **thin**: move data shaping, parsing, guards, and branching into **pure helpers** or **hooks**, not duplicated inline blocks.
- **Do not repeat** the same non-trivial logic in multiple places: extract a **named helper** (or hook) the first time the pattern appears again. Prefer `src/lib/*` or feature-local `*Helpers.ts` for shared logic.
- Helpers should be **small, typed, and testable**; add **`*.test.ts`** when behavior is easy to get wrong.

## Comments

- **Do not put comments in code** — no `//`, no `/* */`, and **no JSDoc/TSDoc** (or any other comment syntax). Explain intent with **names, types, structure, and tests** instead.
- When editing a file, **do not add** new comments. Avoid drive-by comment deletion unless you are removing the code the comment referred to.

## Imports

Prefer `@/` imports over deep relative paths when crossing feature boundaries.

## Column keys and string helpers

- Use shared column constants/enums (for example `REVIEW_COLUMN.FLAG_REASON`) instead of hardcoded column name strings.
- When introducing new domain/status/column keys used in app logic, define or reuse shared enums/constants immediately instead of adding new raw string literals.
- When checking string presence/emptiness, centralize checks in shared helpers instead of ad-hoc inline expressions.
- Prefer explicit `!== undefined` checks after helper normalization when branching on optional string values.

## Commits

Follow repository commit hooks and message conventions; do not bypass hooks.

## Tests

- Prefer role-based queries in RTL tests; use stable data attributes only when needed.
- Keep API-dependent tests aligned with current mock strategy so tests do not hit real endpoints.
